{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b5bd7621f258ed2c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "148082 paper_html.html\n<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<title>Extracting Voice Styles from Frozen TTS Models via Gradient-Based Inverse Optimization</title>\n<!--Generated on Tue Jul 28 06:48:11 2026 by LaTeXML (version 0.8.8) http://dlmf.nist.gov/LaTeXML/.-->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no\">\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/css/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.css\" type=\"text/css\"\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "82477f26-2fd9-40b9-a72a-0d587552c79f", "uuid": "7ad84050-3219-4b87-9430-1bade3c1f636", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T07:29:43.694Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "148082 paper_html.html\n<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<title>Extracting Voice Styles from Frozen TTS Models via Gradient-Based Inverse Optimization</title>\n<!--Generated on Tue Jul 28 06:48:11 2026 by LaTeXML (version 0.8.8) http://dlmf.nist.gov/LaTeXML/.-->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no\">\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/css/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.css\" type=\"text/css\"", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8ded00abc7722752", "type": "tool_result", "content": "588 paper_text.txt\nEXTRACTING VOICE STYLES FROM FROZEN TTS MODELS VIA GRADIENT-BASED\nINVERSE OPTIMIZATION\nGyeongmin Kim\nDepartment of Computer Science, Hanyang University, Seoul, Korea\nkdrkdrkdr@hanyang.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2607.25351v1 [eess.AS] 28 Jul 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nSome text-to-speech systems ship a synthesis model and preset style\nvectors but not the reference encoder that turns audio into such a\nvector. The model still accepts a style vector; a user with a voice of\ntheir own cannot produce one. We solve for that input directly, inverting the released pipeline by gradient descent: every weight stays\nfrozen and only the style vector is optimized, against time-pooled\nWavLM statistics of one recording. Because the objective discards\nthe time axis, the synthesized text may differ from the recording,\nso no transcript and no alignment are needed. On 154 speakers from\ntwo corpora, ECAPA-TDNN similarity rises from 0.132 to 0.413 and\nResNet from 0.099 to 0.401, improving for every speaker; a verifier\nat its equal-error point accepts 53% of the recovered voices as the\ntarget, against 1% for the presets they start from.\nIndex Terms— Speaker verification, spoofing, voice cloning,\nmodel inversion, WavLM\n\na pooled statistic in place of an aligned reconstruction is what makes\ntexture and style inversion work on frozen image generators [6, 7].\nOur contributions are:\n1. A method for recovering a withheld conditioning input from\na released synthesizer. Nothing is trained, and no transcript,\nforced aligner, speaker-verification model, enrolled population, or reference encoder is required at any stage.\n2. The objective that makes it possible: a match between timepooled statistics of an early self-supervised layer, taken from\nutterances that say different words. It transfers to speakerverification metrics it never saw, and we characterize the point\npast which it has to be stopped.\n3. Validation on 154 speakers across two corpora that differ in recording condition. Code is released at https:\n//github.com/kdrkdrkdr/supertonic.embed.\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\n2. RELATED WORK\n\nNeural TTS models such as SupertonicTTS [1] synthesize speech\nfar faster than real time at a fraction of the parameter count of earlier systems. Many condition generation on a style vector encoding\nspeaker identity and vocal character, so one model can voice any\nspeaker for which such a vector exists. Obtaining one for a new\nspeaker normally requires a reference encoder that maps audio into\nthe conditioning space.\nSome released systems ship the synthesis model and a handful\nof preset styles but keep that encoder private. The synthesizer still\ntakes a style vector; what is missing is the map from audio to that\nvector. This is an inverse problem rather than a training problem [2]:\nthe function is fixed and public, and the unknown is one of its inputs.\nWe recover the missing input by optimization: given the released pipeline and one target recording, we search for the style vector whose synthesized output matches the target voice. Nothing is\ntrained and nothing about the released system changes. The absent\nencoder puts three things in the way. Gradients must flow through\nan inference graph never meant for training; the objective must measure speaker identity while the generated and target utterances say\ndifferent words; and with no encoder there is no bound on the valid\nstyle region, so the loss cannot simply be driven down.\nFitting a conditioning vector while the generator is held fixed\nis familiar from speaker adaptation [3–5], but those methods extend\na model their authors trained and supervise it by reconstructing the\ntarget’s own transcribed utterance frame by frame. Here the generator is someone else’s inference-only release and the target arrives as\na bare recording. Our objective therefore discards the time axis and\nmatches per-dimension statistics of an early self-supervised layer,\ncontent-independent up to a measurable floor (Section 4.3); putting\n\nCloning and adaptation. Zero-shot systems rely on speaker encoders [8], codec prompting [9], or infilling from an audio context [10]. Closer to us, VoiceLoop [3], the embedding-only route\nof Arik et al. [4], and SEA-Emb [5] hold the generator fixed and fit\na conditioning vector for one named speaker. Two things separate\nthem. The generator is one the authors trained, so its weights and\ncorpus are theirs to use; and the objective reconstructs the target’s\nown transcribed utterance, a per-frame loss that VoiceLoop says\n“requires an exact temporal alignment of the input and the output\nsequence.” SEA-Emb further conditions on linguistic features and\nf0 extracted from the adaptation data, which need a text front-end,\na forced aligner and a pitch tracker. None of those exists for a bare\nrecording, nor is accepted by an inference-only release.\nOptimizing a conditioning vector for something other than a\nvoice. A second line optimizes inside a synthesizer without changing its weights: a style code driven toward transcription error [11],\nor a speaker embedding driven toward endless generation [12]. Neither objective refers to a target voice at all. Marras et al. [13] are\nthe closest neighbor, optimizing a frozen cloner’s conditioning embedding into a reusable vector, but by gradient-free evolution against\nthe mean similarity to a proxy population, in what they call an “untargeted dictionary attack”: it matches many speakers by chance\nrather than reproducing one. Their objective also needs a speakerverification encoder, which ours never uses.\nReaching a voice without a speaker encoder. kNN-VC [14]\nand kNN-TTS [15] reach a chosen voice from untranscribed audio\nand public self-supervised features, with no speaker encoder. Retrieval does the work: the target is kept as a searchable database\nof frames rather than reduced to a vector, and nothing is optimized\n\n\fTarget audio\n\nFrozen TTS pipeline\nText\nencoder\n\nsttl\n\nVector\nestimator\n\nVocoder\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "82477f26-2fd9-40b9-a72a-0d587552c79f", "uuid": "f7a23e73-eee5-4dc1-ae79-8cdbf395a937", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T07:29:43.803Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "588 paper_text.txt\nEXTRACTING VOICE STYLES FROM FROZEN TTS MODELS VIA GRADIENT-BASED\nINVERSE OPTIMIZATION\nGyeongmin Kim\nDepartment of Computer Science, Hanyang University, Seoul, Korea\nkdrkdrkdr@hanyang.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2607.25351v1 [eess.AS] 28 Jul 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nSome text-to-speech systems ship a synthesis model and preset style\nvectors but not the reference encoder that turns audio into such a\nvector. The model still accepts a style vector; a user with a voice of\ntheir own cannot produce one. We solve for that input directly, inverting the released pipeline by gradient descent: every weight stays\nfrozen and only the style vector is optimized, against time-pooled\nWavLM statistics of one recording. Because the objective discards\nthe time axis, the synthesized text may differ from the recording,\nso no transcript and no alignment are needed. On 154 speakers from\ntwo corpora, ECAPA-TDNN similarity rises from 0.132 to 0.413 and\nResNet from 0.099 to 0.401, improving for every speaker; a verifier\nat its equal-error point accepts 53% of the recovered voices as the\ntarget, against 1% for the presets they start from.\nIndex Terms— Speaker verification, spoofing, voice cloning,\nmodel inversion, WavLM\n\na pooled statistic in place of an aligned reconstruction is what makes\ntexture and style inversion work on frozen image generators [6, 7].\nOur contributions are:\n1. A method for recovering a withheld conditioning input from\na released synthesizer. Nothing is trained, and no transcript,\nforced aligner, speaker-verification model, enrolled population, or reference encoder is required at any stage.\n2. The objective that makes it possible: a match between timepooled statistics of an early self-supervised layer, taken from\nutterances that say different words. It transfers to speakerverification metrics it never saw, and we characterize the point\npast which it has to be stopped.\n3. Validation on 154 speakers across two corpora that differ in recording condition. Code is released at https:\n//github.com/kdrkdrkdr/supertonic.embed.\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\n2. RELATED WORK\n\nNeural TTS models such as SupertonicTTS [1] synthesize speech\nfar faster than real time at a fraction of the parameter count of earlier systems. Many condition generation on a style vector encoding\nspeaker identity and vocal character, so one model can voice any\nspeaker for which such a vector exists. Obtaining one for a new\nspeaker normally requires a reference encoder that maps audio into\nthe conditioning space.\nSome released systems ship the synthesis model and a handful\nof preset styles but keep that encoder private. The synthesizer still\ntakes a style vector; what is missing is the map from audio to that\nvector. This is an inverse problem rather than a training problem [2]:\nthe function is fixed and public, and the unknown is one of its inputs.\nWe recover the missing input by optimization: given the released pipeline and one target recording, we search for the style vector whose synthesized output matches the target voice. Nothing is\ntrained and nothing about the released system changes. The absent\nencoder puts three things in the way. Gradients must flow through\nan inference graph never meant for training; the objective must measure speaker identity while the generated and target utterances say\ndifferent words; and with no encoder there is no bound on the valid\nstyle region, so the loss cannot simply be driven down.\nFitting a conditioning vector while the generator is held fixed\nis familiar from speaker adaptation [3–5], but those methods extend\na model their authors trained and supervise it by reconstructing the\ntarget’s own transcribed utterance frame by frame. Here the generator is someone else’s inference-only release and the target arrives as\na bare recording. Our objective therefore discards the time axis and\nmatches per-dimension statistics of an early self-supervised layer,\ncontent-independent up to a measurable floor (Section 4.3); putting\n\nCloning and adaptation. Zero-shot systems rely on speaker encoders [8], codec prompting [9], or infilling from an audio context [10]. Closer to us, VoiceLoop [3], the embedding-only route\nof Arik et al. [4], and SEA-Emb [5] hold the generator fixed and fit\na conditioning vector for one named speaker. Two things separate\nthem. The generator is one the authors trained, so its weights and\ncorpus are theirs to use; and the objective reconstructs the target’s\nown transcribed utterance, a per-frame loss that VoiceLoop says\n“requires an exact temporal alignment of the input and the output\nsequence.” SEA-Emb further conditions on linguistic features and\nf0 extracted from the adaptation data, which need a text front-end,\na forced aligner and a pitch tracker. None of those exists for a bare\nrecording, nor is accepted by an inference-only release.\nOptimizing a conditioning vector for something other than a\nvoice. A second line optimizes inside a synthesizer without changing its weights: a style code driven toward transcription error [11],\nor a speaker embedding driven toward endless generation [12]. Neither objective refers to a target voice at all. Marras et al. [13] are\nthe closest neighbor, optimizing a frozen cloner’s conditioning embedding into a reusable vector, but by gradient-free evolution against\nthe mean similarity to a proxy population, in what they call an “untargeted dictionary attack”: it matches many speakers by chance\nrather than reproducing one. Their objective also needs a speakerverification encoder, which ours never uses.\nReaching a voice without a speaker encoder. kNN-VC [14]\nand kNN-TTS [15] reach a chosen voice from untranscribed audio\nand public self-supervised features, with no speaker encoder. Retrieval does the work: the target is kept as a searchable database\nof frames rather than reduced to a vector, and nothing is optimized\n\n\fTarget audio\n\nFrozen TTS pipeline\nText\nencoder\n\nsttl\n\nVector\nestimator\n\nVocoder", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
