{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a67ada451f71abbe", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tEXTRACTING VOICE STYLES FROM FROZEN TTS MODELS VIA GRADIENT-BASED\n2\tINVERSE OPTIMIZATION\n3\tGyeongmin Kim\n4\tDepartment of Computer Science, Hanyang University, Seoul, Korea\n5\tkdrkdrkdr@hanyang.ac.kr\n6\t\n7\tarXiv:2607.25351v1 [eess.AS] 28 Jul 2026\n8\t\n9\tABSTRACT\n10\tSome text-to-speech systems ship a synthesis model and preset style\n11\tvectors but not the reference encoder that turns audio into such a\n12\tvector. The model still accepts a style vector; a user with a voice of\n13\ttheir own cannot produce one. We solve for that input directly, inverting the released pipeline by gradient descent: every weight stays\n14\tfrozen and only the style vector is optimized, against time-pooled\n15\tWavLM statistics of one recording. Because the objective discards\n16\tthe time axis, the synthesized text may differ from the recording,\n17\tso no transcript and no alignment are needed. On 154 speakers from\n18\ttwo corpora, ECAPA-TDNN similarity rises from 0.132 to 0.413 and\n19\tResNet from 0.099 to 0.401, improving for every speaker; a verifier\n20\tat its equal-error point accepts 53% of the recovered voices as the\n21\ttarget, against 1% for the presets they start from.\n22\tIndex Terms— Speaker verification, spoofing, voice cloning,\n23\tmodel inversion, WavLM\n24\t\n25\ta pooled statistic in place of an aligned reconstruction is what makes\n26\ttexture and style inversion work on frozen image generators [6, 7].\n27\tOur contributions are:\n28\t1. A method for recovering a withheld conditioning input from\n29\ta released synthesizer. Nothing is trained, and no transcript,\n30\tforced aligner, speaker-verification model, enrolled population, or reference encoder is required at any stage.\n31\t2. The objective that makes it possible: a match between timepooled statistics of an early self-supervised layer, taken from\n32\tutterances that say different words. It transfers to speakerverification metrics it never saw, and we characterize the point\n33\tpast which it has to be stopped.\n34\t3. Validation on 154 speakers across two corpora that differ in recording condition. Code is released at https:\n35\t//github.com/kdrkdrkdr/supertonic.embed.\n36\t\n37\t1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t\n39\t2. RELATED WORK\n40\t\n41\tNeural TTS models such as SupertonicTTS [1] synthesize speech\n42\tfar faster than real time at a fraction of the parameter count of earlier systems. Many condition generation on a style vector encoding\n43\tspeaker identity and vocal character, so one model can voice any\n44\tspeaker for which such a vector exists. Obtaining one for a new\n45\tspeaker normally requires a reference encoder that maps audio into\n46\tthe conditioning space.\n47\tSome released systems ship the synthesis model and a handful\n48\tof preset styles but keep that encoder private. The synthesizer still\n49\ttakes a style vector; what is missing is the map from audio to that\n50\tvector. This is an inverse problem rather than a training problem [2]:\n51\tthe function is fixed and public, and the unknown is one of its inputs.\n52\tWe recover the missing input by optimization: given the released pipeline and one target recording, we search for the style vector whose synthesized output matches the target voice. Nothing is\n53\ttrained and nothing about the released system changes. The absent\n54\tencoder puts three things in the way. Gradients must flow through\n55\tan inference graph never meant for training; the objective must measure speaker identity while the generated and target utterances say\n56\tdifferent words; and with no encoder there is no bound on the valid\n57\tstyle region, so the loss cannot simply be driven down.\n58\tFitting a conditioning vector while the generator is held fixed\n59\tis familiar from speaker adaptation [3–5], but those methods extend\n60\ta model their authors trained and supervise it by reconstructing the\n61\ttarget’s own transcribed utterance frame by frame. Here the generator is someone else’s inference-only release and the target arrives as\n62\ta bare recording. Our objective therefore discards the time axis and\n63\tmatches per-dimension statistics of an early self-supervised layer,\n64\tcontent-independent up to a measurable floor (Section 4.3); putting\n65\t\n66\tCloning and adaptation. Zero-shot systems rely on speaker encoders [8], codec prompting [9], or infilling from an audio context [10]. Closer to us, VoiceLoop [3], the embedding-only route\n67\tof Arik et al. [4], and SEA-Emb [5] hold the generator fixed and fit\n68\ta conditioning vector for one named speaker. Two things separate\n69\tthem. The generator is one the authors trained, so its weights and\n70\tcorpus are theirs to use; and the objective reconstructs the target’s\n71\town transcribed utterance, a per-frame loss that VoiceLoop says\n72\t“requires an exact temporal alignment of the input and the output\n73\tsequence.” SEA-Emb further conditions on linguistic features and\n74\tf0 extracted from the adaptation data, which need a text front-end,\n75\ta forced aligner and a pitch tracker. None of those exists for a bare\n76\trecording, nor is accepted by an inference-only release.\n77\tOptimizing a conditioning vector for something other than a\n78\tvoice. A second line optimizes inside a synthesizer without changing its weights: a style code driven toward transcription error [11],\n79\tor a speaker embedding driven toward endless generation [12]. Neither objective refers to a target voice at all. Marras et al. [13] are\n80\tthe closest neighbor, optimizing a frozen cloner’s conditioning embedding into a reusable vector, but by gradient-free evolution against\n81\tthe mean similarity to a proxy population, in what they call an “untargeted dictionary attack”: it matches many speakers by chance\n82\trather than reproducing one. Their objective also needs a speakerverification encoder, which ours never uses.\n83\tReaching a voice without a speaker encoder. kNN-VC [14]\n84\tand kNN-TTS [15] reach a chosen voice from untranscribed audio\n85\tand public self-supervised features, with no speaker encoder. Retrieval does the work: the target is kept as a searchable database\n86\tof frames rather than reduced to a vector, and nothing is optimized\n87\t\n88\t\fTarget audio\n89\t\n90\tFrozen TTS pipeline\n91\tText\n92\tencoder\n93\t\n94\tsttl\n95\t\n96\tVector\n97\testimator\n98\t\n99\tVocoder\n100\t\n101\tGenerated\n102\taudio\n103\t\n104\tWavLM\n105\tlayer 4\n106\t\n107\tL (µ, σ )\n108\t\n109\t∇sttl L\n110\tFig. 1. Only the style vector sttl (dark) receives gradients; every module of the TTS pipeline and of WavLM stays frozen. The loss compares\n111\ttime-pooled layer-4 statistics, so the generated and target audio may carry different text.\n112\t\n113\ttoward it. kNN-VC, which calls itself non-parametric, reports that\n114\tbelow roughly 30 s of target speech “the more complex baselines\n115\tperform better.” Aldeneh et al. [16] use the same time-pooled mean\n116\tand standard deviation of a frozen self-supervised layer that our loss\n117\tregresses onto, but as a similarity score read between two real recordings, never as a quantity anything is optimized toward.\n118\tLatent optimization. GAN inversion [17] recovers latent codes\n119\tthrough a frozen generator and Textual Inversion [18] optimizes embeddings in diffusion models. The image domain already substitutes\n120\ta pooled statistic for the aligned term where alignment is meaningless: Image2StyleGAN++ [6] optimizes a latent with its pixel losses\n121\tdisabled, and Lin et al. [7] refine real-texture inversions against a\n122\tGramian loss, having judged location-wise losses ill suited to textures. Audio style transfer [19] matches such statistics too, but optimizes the output signal rather than an input to a fixed generator.\n123\tLayer structure. Self-supervised speech models distribute information unevenly with depth [20]; a layer-wise probe of WavLMLarge puts peak speaker-identification accuracy at layer 4 [21].\n124\t3. METHOD\n125\tThe target deployment pattern is a TTS release that publishes the\n126\tsynthesis model and a set of preset style vectors while keeping the\n127\treference encoder private. Few public systems fit it: most either\n128\tship the speaker encoder alongside the weights (CosyVoice [22]),\n129\tprompt directly on reference audio with no explicit style vector (F5TTS [23]), or condition on discrete codec tokens (VALL-E [9]). We\n130\tstudy SupertonicTTS, which fits the pattern and is small enough to\n131\tdifferentiate through end to end. Fig. 1 shows the setup.\n132\t3.1. Problem formulation\n133\tLet G be a frozen TTS pipeline mapping text t and a style vector s to\n134\ta waveform. For a target recording y∗ we solve\n135\t\u0001\n136\ts∗ = arg min L G(t, s), y∗ ,\n137\t(1)\n138\ts\n139\t\n140\twhere t is text of our choosing, unrelated to what y∗ says. SupertonicTTS exposes sttl ∈ R50×256 , which enters the acoustic modules as cross-attention keys and values, and a separate duration style\n141\tsdp ∈ R8×16 . We optimize sttl , 12,800 free parameters, and keep sdp\n142\tfixed.\n143\t\n144\t3.3. A content-independent objective\n145\tThe generated and target utterances contain different words, so the\n146\tobjective must ignore content. Let H = [h1 , . . . , hT ] ∈ RT ×D denote\n147\tWavLM-Large layer-4 hidden states, D=1024. We collapse the time\n148\taxis into per-dimension statistics,\n149\tµ = T1 ∑τ hτ ,\n150\t\n151\tσ = std(H),\n152\t\n153\t(2)\n154\t\n155\tand compare them by mean squared error,\n156\t2\n157\t\n158\t2\n159\t\n160\tL = D1 µ ŷ − µ y∗ 2 + D1 σ ŷ − σ y∗ 2 .\n161\t\n162\t(3)\n163\t\n164\tMean-and-standard-deviation pooling over time is the statistics pooling used by x-vector speaker embeddings [24]. More directly, this\n165\texact descriptor (time-pooled mean and standard deviation of frozen\n166\tSSL hidden states, no trained head) is itself a competent speaker\n167\trepresentation [16], which is why the objective transfers to speaker\n168\tverification metrics it never saw. Pooling over time also removes\n169\tany dependence on utterance length. We use layer 4, where Chiu et\n170\tal. [21] report peak speaker-identification accuracy for this model;\n171\ttheir layer indexing counts the CNN encoder output as layer 0 and\n172\tso matches ours.\n173\t3.4. Initialization and stopping\n174\tOptimization starts from the preset whose output on the first prompt\n175\tis closest to the target under Eq. 3, over the ten released presets.\n176\tBecause the encoder is unavailable, the extent of the valid style\n177\tregion is unknown, and driving the loss arbitrarily low pushes the\n178\tvector into territory the model was never trained on. We therefore\n179\tcalibrate a stopping point from public material only. Synthesizing\n180\ttwo different sentences from the same preset leaves a residual loss\n181\tattributable to content rather than to speaker mismatch. Across the\n182\tten presets and four prompts, these 60 same-preset pairs span 0.15 to\n183\t0.41 with a mean of 0.27. We stop at 0.30; 37 of the 60 pairs fall below that value, so the stopping point sits near the 62nd percentile of\n184\tthe content-only floor. This floor is measured between two synthesized clips, while optimization compares a synthesized clip to a real\n185\tone, and the two are not on the same scale: 0.30 is a calibrated operating point, not a bound on where content ceases to be the residual.\n186\tSection 4.3 shows what happens on either side of it.\n187\t\n188\t3.2. Gradient flow through a deployed graph\n189\tThe released pipeline is four ONNX modules built for inference. We\n190\tsimplify each graph with onnxslim, downgrade the opset from 19\n191\tto 17, and strip trailing empty inputs from Clip operators, after which\n192\tonnx2torch produces a differentiable PyTorch module. Every converted parameter and every WavLM parameter is frozen; the style\n193\tvector is the only tensor in the optimizer.\n194\t\n195\t3.5. Optimization details\n196\tFive English prompts chosen for phoneme coverage rotate across\n197\tsteps so the style vector cannot overfit one phoneme set. We use\n198\tAdam at learning rate 2×10−4 , gradient clipping at max-norm 1.0,\n199\tand ReduceLROnPlateau with patience 200 and factor 0.5. Each\n200\tprompt has its own noise latent, drawn once from a fixed seed and\n201\t\n202\t\fTable 1. 154 speakers, five utterances each. The baseline is the\n203\tpreset each speaker was initialized from, synthesized on the same\n204\tprompts.\n205\tSIME ↑ SIMR ↑ SIMW ↑ WER↓\n206\tAll (154)\n207\t\n208\tPreset\n209\tProposed\n210\t\n211\t0.132\n212\t0.413\n213\t\n214\t0.099\n215\t0.401\n216\t\n217\t0.713\n218\t0.836\n219\t\n220\t1.84%\n221\t3.19%\n222\t\n223\tVCTK (110)\n224\t\n225\tPreset\n226\tProposed\n227\t\n228\t0.132\n229\t0.393\n230\t\n231\t0.099\n232\t0.384\n233\t\n234\t0.699\n235\t0.824\n236\t\n237\t1.67%\n238\t3.99%\n239\t\n240\tSeed-TTS (44)\n241\t\n242\tPreset\n243\tProposed\n244\t\n245\t0.132\n246\t0.463\n247\t\n248\t0.099\n249\t0.444\n250\t\n251\t0.747\n252\t0.865\n253\t\n254\t2.28%\n255\t1.19%\n256\t\n257\treused at every step. That latent is sized once, from a single preset’s predicted duration, and reused for every speaker, so the duration predictor’s output never enters the waveform path and sdp has\n258\tno gradient path to the loss: utterance timing is not recovered here.\n259\tBecause each style vector reaches only its own output, several\n260\tspeakers can be optimized in one forward pass by summing their\n261\tper-speaker losses; a speaker that reaches the threshold is removed\n262\tfrom the batch at once, so it cannot drift past the stopping point.\n263\tAt batch 16 on one RTX 3090 this costs 0.49 minutes per speaker,\n264\tagainst 5 to 6 minutes optimized singly.\n265\t4. EXPERIMENTS\n266\t4.1. Setup\n267\tWe evaluate the public SupertonicTTS 2 release [25] (internal version v1.6.0), whose architecture is described in [1]. It ships 10\n268\tpreset styles and no reference encoder.\n269\tTargets are 154 speakers from two corpora with different recording conditions: 110 from VCTK [26] (studio, 8 to 12 s of reference\n270\taudio, real gender and accent metadata) and 44 from the Common\n271\tVoice subset of Seed-TTS Eval [27] (crowdsourced, 3 to 16 s). For\n272\tevery speaker we synthesize the same five prompts used during optimization, from the extracted style and from the preset it started\n273\tfrom, so the baseline is matched. The target recording’s own words\n274\tare never used at any stage.\n275\tSpeaker similarity is cosine similarity between reference and\n276\tgenerated embeddings under ECAPA-TDNN [28] (SIME ) and\n277\tResNet (SIMR ), both from SpeechBrain VoxCeleb checkpoints,\n278\tand under WavLM-base-plus-sv (SIMW ), the metric most TTS work\n279\treports. SIME and SIMR are trained independently of anything we\n280\toptimize; SIMW shares the WavLM family with our objective, so\n281\twe report it for comparability rather than as an independent check.\n282\tIntelligibility is word error rate under Whisper-large-v3.\n283\tFor scale, we measured both ends of the ECAPA range on these\n284\tsame references. Different speakers score 0.118 on average (95th\n285\tpercentile 0.321, maximum 0.629); two recordings of one VCTK\n286\tspeaker score a median of 0.682; two halves of a single recording,\n287\twhich also fixes the channel, score 0.804 over the 121 references\n288\tlong enough to split. The nearest preset therefore starts almost at the\n289\timpostor floor. The impostor tail is long, so a single score near 0.4\n290\tis not by itself proof of identity; the comparison that matters is the\n291\tpaired change per speaker.\n292\t4.2. Main results\n293\tTable 1 reports the result against the nearest-preset baseline, the\n294\tstrongest option available to a user restricted to the public release.\n295\t\n296\tSpeaker similarity rises from the impostor floor to about 60% of\n297\tthe 0.682 that two real recordings of one speaker score: SIME from\n298\t0.132 to 0.413 and SIMR from 0.099 to 0.401. Bootstrap 95% confidence intervals over speakers are [0.395, 0.430] and [0.385, 0.418].\n299\tThe gain is universal, not an average over successes and failures.\n300\tEvery one of the 154 speakers ends closer to its target than its own\n301\tpreset does, under SIME and SIMR , and 145 of 154 under SIMW .\n302\tThe objective sees none of these three, so the gain is transfer, not the\n303\toptimizer scoring its own work. A Wilcoxon signed-rank test returns\n304\tp < 10−26 for both independent metrics, with paired effect sizes dz\n305\tof 2.70 and 2.82. The two verification models also rank speakers\n306\talmost identically (Spearman ρ = 0.881), so the improvement is not\n307\tan artifact of one embedding space.\n308\tExtraction costs intelligibility but does not destroy it: mean\n309\tWER rises from 1.84% to 3.19%. The mean is misleading here.\n310\tThe median is 0.00% and 107 of the 154 speakers transcribe with no\n311\terror at all, so the increase is carried by a minority of failures rather\n312\tthan by uniform degradation. Section 4.3 attributes part of that tail\n313\tto the synthesizer itself.\n314\tThe recovered vectors also behave like native ones. Predicted\n315\tnaturalness (UTMOS [29]) reaches 4.23 against 4.47 for the model’s\n316\town presets, 95% of what the synthesizer attains from a style vector\n317\tit shipped; the median speaker gives up 0.10, at dz = 0.66 against\n318\t2.70 for the similarity gain on the same clips. Staying near the preset\n319\tlevel is evidence that the vector lands inside the region the model\n320\twas trained on, which is what the stopping rule buys (Section 4.3).\n321\tUTMOS is trained on systems from earlier challenges, so it bounds\n322\tthis rather than measuring naturalness.\n323\tThe two corpora differ: Seed-TTS speakers reach higher similarity (0.463 against 0.393) and their WER falls rather than rises,\n324\twhile VCTK speakers gain less despite converging faster (297 steps\n325\tagainst 568). They differ in recording condition, duration, and accent coverage at once, so we offer no controlled explanation.\n326\t4.3. Where to stop\n327\tThe stopping threshold is the one hyperparameter with no principled\n328\tsetting available from the release alone, so we characterize what\n329\tit buys. Continuing six speakers from 0.30 down to 0.24, below\n330\tthe calibrated stopping point, raises SIME from 0.498 to 0.534 but\n331\tlifts word error rate from 1.07% to 5.22% and leaves SIMW slightly\n332\tlower, 0.910 against 0.906. Past that point the optimizer is buying\n333\tspeaker similarity with intelligibility. Two of the six speakers account for nearly all the damage, one going from 0.00% to 18.85%\n334\tWER.\n335\tSome of that fragility is the synthesizer’s, not ours. Synthesizing\n336\tthe ten stock presets with no optimization at all, over 250 clips, gives\n337\ta mean WER of 0.38%, but two clips exceed 10% and one presetsentence pair swings 15 points across noise seeds. Any single-clip\n338\tWER from this model, including ours, carries that variance.\n339\t4.4. Would a verifier accept these voices?\n340\tA cosine similarity is hard to read as a risk. Table 2 restates the result\n341\tas a verification decision, in the form a spoofing evaluation would\n342\tuse: with the verifier tuned to a given false-accept rate on genuine\n343\tand impostor trials, how often does it accept an extracted voice as\n344\tthe target speaker? On this data the verifier separates real speakers\n345\tcleanly, with an equal-error rate of 0.9% at a threshold of 0.406.\n346\tAt that operating point it accepts 52.6% of the extracted voices and\n347\t1.3% of the presets they were built from. Loosening to a 5% falseaccept rate raises acceptance to 85.7%, against 9.1% for the presets.\n348\t\n349\t\f(a) convergence, 154 speakers\n350\t\n351\t0.8\n352\t\n353\tspeakers\n354\t\n355\tWavLM layer-4 loss\n356\t\n357\t1.0\n358\t\n359\t0.6\n360\tmean\n361\t\n362\t0.4\n363\t0.2\n364\t\n365\t0\n366\t\n367\t200\n368\t\n369\t400\n370\t\n371\tstopping threshold 0.3\n372\t\n373\t600\n374\t800\n375\toptimization step\n376\t\n377\t1000\n378\t\n379\t(b) speaker similarity, 154 speakers\n380\t\n381\t30\n382\t\n383\t1200\n384\t\n385\tnearest preset\n386\tproposed\n387\t\n388\t20\n389\t10\n390\t0\n391\t\n392\t0.0\n393\t\n394\t0.2\n395\t0.4\n396\tSIME (ECAPA-TDNN)\n397\t\n398\t0.6\n399\t\n400\tFig. 2. (a) Best-so-far loss for all 154 speakers; light curves are individual speakers, the dark curve their mean. Every speaker reaches the\n401\t0.30 threshold, on average after 375 steps and between 80 and 1275. (b) ECAPA similarity before and after extraction; the two distributions\n402\tbarely overlap.\n403\t\n404\tTable 2. Acceptance by an ECAPA verifier tuned to a given falseaccept rate. “Genuine” is a second real recording of the same\n405\tspeaker, which is what a deployed verifier actually sees; those trials\n406\tcover the 110 VCTK speakers, the only ones with a second recording, against 6000 impostor pairs over all 154 references.\n407\tOperating point\n408\t\n409\tThresh.\n410\t\n411\tGenuine\n412\t\n413\tExtracted\n414\t\n415\tPreset\n416\t\n417\tEER (0.9%)\n418\tFAR = 5%\n419\tFAR = 1%\n420\tFAR = 0.1%\n421\t\n422\t0.406\n423\t0.290\n424\t0.399\n425\t0.519\n426\t\n427\t99.1%\n428\t100.0%\n429\t100.0%\n430\t92.7%\n431\t\n432\t52.6%\n433\t85.7%\n434\t53.9%\n435\t18.8%\n436\t\n437\t1.3%\n438\t9.1%\n439\t1.9%\n440\t0.0%\n441\t\n442\t4.5. How accurate is preset selection?\n443\tVCTK ships per-speaker gender labels, which lets us score the initialization against ground truth rather than against itself. The nearest\n444\tpreset matches the labeled gender for 105 of 110 speakers (95.5%).\n445\tAll five errors share one direction: a male preset (M1) chosen for\n446\ta female speaker. Initialization is therefore good but not reliable,\n447\twhich matters because the starting point is the one part of the procedure with no feedback signal.\n448\t5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n449\tWhat initialization does. Preset initialization is a convenience, not\n450\ta requirement. In an early three-speaker pilot, run at layer 3 with\n451\tthree prompts and a 0.24 stopping loss, a zero vector reaches SIME\n452\t0.496 against 0.594 for the standard start; on the two speakers also\n453\trun from an opposite-category preset, the mean falls from 0.593 to\n454\t0.460. All are far above the 0.13 baseline, so optimization recovers\n455\tfrom a poor start rather than failing. That pilot predates the settings\n456\tabove and scores above the 154-speaker mean, so only its internal\n457\tordering is meaningful.\n458\tLayer choice. We adopt the layer that [21] reports as best for\n459\tspeaker identity rather than sweeping layers ourselves. Neighboring\n460\tearly layers plausibly work as well; the loss scale differs between\n461\tthem, so each would need its own stopping threshold.\n462\tDuration style. The latent length is fixed in our graph, so sdp\n463\thas no gradient path to the loss and speaking rate is inherited from\n464\tthe initializing preset. Recovering it needs a latent whose length\n465\tfollows the duration predictor, which we leave open.\n466\t\n467\tNo countermeasure evaluation. We measure acceptance by a\n468\tspeaker verifier, not by a spoofing countermeasure. Synthetic speech\n469\tfrom this model may well be detectable even when the verifier accepts it: our result bounds what identity information the conditioning space carries, not whether the audio survives a detector. Pairing\n470\textraction with a countermeasure benchmark is the natural next experiment.\n471\tScope. Results come from one TTS model. The method assumes only a differentiable pipeline and a continuous style input, so\n472\tit should transfer, but we have not shown that. The five evaluation\n473\tprompts are the ones optimized on, so generalization to unseen text\n474\tis untested. Naturalness is only a predicted score; a listening test remains necessary. Accent coverage is uneven and the weakest results\n475\tfall on the least represented accents.\n476\t6. CONCLUSION\n477\tWe recover a withheld conditioning input from a released synthesizer by optimization alone. Without an encoder there is no supervision signal for the fit; we substitute a time-pooled early-layer\n478\tWavLM statistic, which makes the comparison content-independent,\n479\tso one untranscribed recording suffices and it need not say anything in particular. A stopping point calibrated from the released\n480\tpresets keeps the result inside the region the model was trained on.\n481\tAcross 154 speakers the method raises ECAPA speaker similarity\n482\tfrom 0.132 to 0.413, improves every speaker, and costs half a minute\n483\tof GPU time each; a verifier at its equal-error point accepts 53% of\n484\tthe results as the target, against 1% for the presets they were derived\n485\tfrom.\n486\tOne implication is that withholding the encoder removes a convenience rather than a capability. Whatever a partial release is meant\n487\tto achieve, it should be assessed against what the released components already determine, not against the intended interface alone.\n488\t7. COMPLIANCE WITH ETHICAL STANDARDS\n489\tUse of a released model outside its intended interface. This work\n490\toperates a publicly released model in a way its authors did not design for: SupertonicTTS ships synthesis weights and preset voices,\n491\tthe reference encoder is withheld, and we reach the conditioning\n492\tspace without it. We used only public artifacts, breached no access\n493\t\n494\t\fcontrol, and modified no released file, but operating a release outside its intended interface carries obligations beyond its license, so\n495\twe state plainly what we did.\n496\tDual use. The measurement is directly usable to imitate a real\n497\tperson. That is not a side effect: the quantity we report is how convincingly a recovered vector reproduces a specific voice, so a high\n498\tnumber is what makes misuse feasible. We report it because that\n499\tsame number tells a provider, or a user relying on one, how much\n500\tof a voice a partial release already determines. A result of this kind\n501\tcannot be reported in a form that informs the defense but not the\n502\toffense.\n503\tData, approval and funding. All evaluation uses consented,\n504\topenly licensed corpora: VCTK (CC BY 4.0) and the Common\n505\tVoice subset of Seed-TTS Eval (CC0). The study was conducted\n506\tretrospectively on human-subject speech data already available in\n507\topen access; no new recordings were collected, and ethical approval\n508\twas not required, as confirmed by the licenses attached to that data.\n509\tNo identifiable individual is targeted, no public figure appears, and\n510\twe release no extracted style vector for any real person. The released\n511\tcode documents that cloning a voice without the speaker’s consent\n512\tis out of scope for it. This work received no external funding, and\n513\tthe author declares no conflict of interest.\n514\tWhat we release. We publish the procedure in enough detail to\n515\treproduce the measurement, with the code that produced the numbers. We do not package it as a turnkey cloning tool, and the technique it builds on has been public since 2018 [3–5], so the marginal\n516\tcapability this adds is small relative to the transparency it buys.\n517\tMitigations available to providers. The conditioning space is\n518\treachable because it is continuous, differentiable, and shipped alongside working examples. Perturbing or quantizing the released presets, watermarking synthesizer output so generated speech remains\n519\tattributable, or serving the acoustic modules behind an interface that\n520\tdoes not expose gradients would each raise the cost. We measured\n521\tnone of these and offer them as directions, not results.\n522\t8. REFERENCES\n523\t[1] H. Kim, J. Yang, Y. Yu, S. Ji, J. Morton, F. Bous, J. Byun, and\n524\tJ. Lee, “SupertonicTTS: Towards highly efficient and streamlined text-to-speech system,” arXiv:2503.23108, 2025.\n525\t[2] M. Fredrikson, S. Jha, and T. Ristenpart, “Model inversion\n526\tattacks that exploit confidence information and basic countermeasures,” in Proc. ACM CCS, 2015, pp. 1322–1333.\n527\t[3] Y. Taigman et al., “VoiceLoop: Voice fitting and synthesis via\n528\ta phonological loop,” in Proc. ICLR, 2018.\n529\t[4] S. Ö. Arik et al., “Neural voice cloning with a few samples,”\n530\tin Proc. NeurIPS, 2018, pp. 10040–10050.\n531\t[5] Y. Chen et al., “Sample efficient adaptive text-to-speech,” in\n532\tProc. ICLR, 2019.\n533\t[6] R. Abdal, Y. Qin, and P. Wonka, “Image2StyleGAN++: How\n534\tto edit the embedded images?,” in Proc. CVPR, 2020, pp.\n535\t8296–8305.\n536\t[7] J. Lin, G. Sharma, and T. N. Pappas, “Texture representation via analysis and synthesis with generative adversarial networks,” arXiv:2212.09983, 2022.\n537\t[8] Y. Jia et al., “Transfer learning from speaker verification\n538\tto multispeaker text-to-speech synthesis,” in Proc. NeurIPS,\n539\t2018, pp. 4485–4495.\n540\t[9] C. Wang et al., “Neural codec language models are zero-shot\n541\ttext to speech synthesizers,” arXiv:2301.02111, 2023.\n542\t\n543\t[10] M. Le et al., “Voicebox: Text-guided multilingual universal\n544\tspeech generation at scale,” in Proc. NeurIPS, 2023.\n545\t[11] W. Jin et al., “Towards evaluating the robustness of automatic speech recognition systems via audio style transfer,”\n546\tarXiv:2405.09470, 2024.\n547\t[12] X. Gao et al., “TTSlow: Slow down text-to-speech with efficiency robustness evaluations,” arXiv:2407.01927, 2024.\n548\t[13] M. Marras et al., “Dictionary attacks on speaker verification,” IEEE Trans. Inf. Forensics Security, vol. 18, pp. 773–\n549\t788, 2023.\n550\t[14] M. Baas, B. van Niekerk, and H. Kamper, “Voice conversion\n551\twith just nearest neighbors,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2023, pp.\n552\t2053–2057.\n553\t[15] K. El Hajal et al., “kNN retrieval for simple and effective zeroshot multi-speaker text-to-speech,” in Proc. NAACL, 2025, pp.\n554\t778–786.\n555\t[16] Z. Aldeneh et al., “Can you remove the downstream model for\n556\tspeaker recognition with self-supervised speech features?,” in\n557\tProc. Interspeech, 2024, pp. 4648–4652.\n558\t[17] R. Abdal, Y. Qin, and P. Wonka, “Image2StyleGAN: How\n559\tto embed images into the StyleGAN latent space?,” in Proc.\n560\tICCV, 2019, pp. 4432–4441.\n561\t[18] R. Gal et al., “An image is worth one word: Personalizing textto-image generation using textual inversion,” in Proc. ICLR,\n562\t2023.\n563\t[19] E. Grinstein, N. Q. K. Duong, A. Ozerov, and P. Pérez, “Audio\n564\tstyle transfer,” in Proc. ICASSP, 2018, pp. 586–590.\n565\t[20] A. Pasad, J.-C. Chou, and K. Livescu, “Layer-wise analysis of\n566\ta self-supervised speech representation model,” in Proc. IEEE\n567\tASRU, 2021, pp. 914–921.\n568\t[21] A. Y. F. Chiu et al., “A large-scale probing analysis of speakerspecific attributes in self-supervised speech representations,”\n569\tarXiv:2501.05310v3, 2026.\n570\t[22] Z. Du et al., “CosyVoice: A scalable multilingual zero-shot\n571\ttext-to-speech synthesizer based on supervised semantic tokens,” arXiv:2407.05407, 2024.\n572\t[23] Y. Chen et al., “F5-TTS: A fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching,” in Proc. ACL, 2025, pp. 6255–\n573\t6271.\n574\t[24] D. Snyder et al., “X-vectors: Robust DNN embeddings for\n575\tspeaker recognition,” in Proc. ICASSP, 2018, pp. 5329–5333.\n576\t[25] Supertone Inc.,\n577\t“Supertonic 2 open-weight release,”\n578\t2026. [Online]. Available: https://huggingface.co/\n579\tSupertone/supertonic-2.\n580\t[26] J. Yamagishi, C. Veaux, and K. MacDonald, “CSTR VCTK\n581\tcorpus: English multi-speaker corpus for CSTR voice cloning\n582\ttoolkit (version 0.92),” Univ. of Edinburgh, 2019.\n583\t[27] P. Anastassiou et al., “Seed-TTS: A family of high-quality\n584\tversatile speech generation models,” arXiv:2406.02430, 2024.\n585\t[28] B. Desplanques, J. Thienpondt, and K. Demuynck, “ECAPATDNN: Emphasized channel attention, propagation and aggregation in TDNN based speaker verification,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2020, pp. 3830–3834.\n586\t[29] T. Saeki et al., “UTMOS: UTokyo-SaruLab system for VoiceMOS Challenge 2022,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2022, pp. 4521–\n587\t4525.\n588\t\n589\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "82477f26-2fd9-40b9-a72a-0d587552c79f", "uuid": "86de5908-29d3-41b6-bffb-cb0f6e295305", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T07:34:44.775Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "EXTRACTING VOICE STYLES FROM FROZEN TTS MODELS VIA GRADIENT-BASED\nINVERSE OPTIMIZATION\nGyeongmin Kim\nDepartment of Computer Science, Hanyang University, Seoul, Korea\nkdrkdrkdr@hanyang.ac.kr\n\narXiv:2607.25351v1 [eess.AS] 28 Jul 2026\n\nABSTRACT\nSome text-to-speech systems ship a synthesis model and preset style\nvectors but not the reference encoder that turns audio into such a\nvector. The model still accepts a style vector; a user with a voice of\ntheir own cannot produce one. We solve for that input directly, inverting the released pipeline by gradient descent: every weight stays\nfrozen and only the style vector is optimized, against time-pooled\nWavLM statistics of one recording. Because the objective discards\nthe time axis, the synthesized text may differ from the recording,\nso no transcript and no alignment are needed. On 154 speakers from\ntwo corpora, ECAPA-TDNN similarity rises from 0.132 to 0.413 and\nResNet from 0.099 to 0.401, improving for every speaker; a verifier\nat its equal-error point accepts 53% of the recovered voices as the\ntarget, against 1% for the presets they start from.\nIndex Terms— Speaker verification, spoofing, voice cloning,\nmodel inversion, WavLM\n\na pooled statistic in place of an aligned reconstruction is what makes\ntexture and style inversion work on frozen image generators [6, 7].\nOur contributions are:\n1. A method for recovering a withheld conditioning input from\na released synthesizer. Nothing is trained, and no transcript,\nforced aligner, speaker-verification model, enrolled population, or reference encoder is required at any stage.\n2. The objective that makes it possible: a match between timepooled statistics of an early self-supervised layer, taken from\nutterances that say different words. It transfers to speakerverification metrics it never saw, and we characterize the point\npast which it has to be stopped.\n3. Validation on 154 speakers across two corpora that differ in recording condition. Code is released at https:\n//github.com/kdrkdrkdr/supertonic.embed.\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\n2. RELATED WORK\n\nNeural TTS models such as SupertonicTTS [1] synthesize speech\nfar faster than real time at a fraction of the parameter count of earlier systems. Many condition generation on a style vector encoding\nspeaker identity and vocal character, so one model can voice any\nspeaker for which such a vector exists. Obtaining one for a new\nspeaker normally requires a reference encoder that maps audio into\nthe conditioning space.\nSome released systems ship the synthesis model and a handful\nof preset styles but keep that encoder private. The synthesizer still\ntakes a style vector; what is missing is the map from audio to that\nvector. This is an inverse problem rather than a training problem [2]:\nthe function is fixed and public, and the unknown is one of its inputs.\nWe recover the missing input by optimization: given the released pipeline and one target recording, we search for the style vector whose synthesized output matches the target voice. Nothing is\ntrained and nothing about the released system changes. The absent\nencoder puts three things in the way. Gradients must flow through\nan inference graph never meant for training; the objective must measure speaker identity while the generated and target utterances say\ndifferent words; and with no encoder there is no bound on the valid\nstyle region, so the loss cannot simply be driven down.\nFitting a conditioning vector while the generator is held fixed\nis familiar from speaker adaptation [3–5], but those methods extend\na model their authors trained and supervise it by reconstructing the\ntarget’s own transcribed utterance frame by frame. Here the generator is someone else’s inference-only release and the target arrives as\na bare recording. Our objective therefore discards the time axis and\nmatches per-dimension statistics of an early self-supervised layer,\ncontent-independent up to a measurable floor (Section 4.3); putting\n\nCloning and adaptation. Zero-shot systems rely on speaker encoders [8], codec prompting [9], or infilling from an audio context [10]. Closer to us, VoiceLoop [3], the embedding-only route\nof Arik et al. [4], and SEA-Emb [5] hold the generator fixed and fit\na conditioning vector for one named speaker. Two things separate\nthem. The generator is one the authors trained, so its weights and\ncorpus are theirs to use; and the objective reconstructs the target’s\nown transcribed utterance, a per-frame loss that VoiceLoop says\n“requires an exact temporal alignment of the input and the output\nsequence.” SEA-Emb further conditions on linguistic features and\nf0 extracted from the adaptation data, which need a text front-end,\na forced aligner and a pitch tracker. None of those exists for a bare\nrecording, nor is accepted by an inference-only release.\nOptimizing a conditioning vector for something other than a\nvoice. A second line optimizes inside a synthesizer without changing its weights: a style code driven toward transcription error [11],\nor a speaker embedding driven toward endless generation [12]. Neither objective refers to a target voice at all. Marras et al. [13] are\nthe closest neighbor, optimizing a frozen cloner’s conditioning embedding into a reusable vector, but by gradient-free evolution against\nthe mean similarity to a proxy population, in what they call an “untargeted dictionary attack”: it matches many speakers by chance\nrather than reproducing one. Their objective also needs a speakerverification encoder, which ours never uses.\nReaching a voice without a speaker encoder. kNN-VC [14]\nand kNN-TTS [15] reach a chosen voice from untranscribed audio\nand public self-supervised features, with no speaker encoder. Retrieval does the work: the target is kept as a searchable database\nof frames rather than reduced to a vector, and nothing is optimized\n\n\fTarget audio\n\nFrozen TTS pipeline\nText\nencoder\n\nsttl\n\nVector\nestimator\n\nVocoder\n\nGenerated\naudio\n\nWavLM\nlayer 4\n\nL (µ, σ )\n\n∇sttl L\nFig. 1. Only the style vector sttl (dark) receives gradients; every module of the TTS pipeline and of WavLM stays frozen. The loss compares\ntime-pooled layer-4 statistics, so the generated and target audio may carry different text.\n\ntoward it. kNN-VC, which calls itself non-parametric, reports that\nbelow roughly 30 s of target speech “the more complex baselines\nperform better.” Aldeneh et al. [16] use the same time-pooled mean\nand standard deviation of a frozen self-supervised layer that our loss\nregresses onto, but as a similarity score read between two real recordings, never as a quantity anything is optimized toward.\nLatent optimization. GAN inversion [17] recovers latent codes\nthrough a frozen generator and Textual Inversion [18] optimizes embeddings in diffusion models. The image domain already substitutes\na pooled statistic for the aligned term where alignment is meaningless: Image2StyleGAN++ [6] optimizes a latent with its pixel losses\ndisabled, and Lin et al. [7] refine real-texture inversions against a\nGramian loss, having judged location-wise losses ill suited to textures. Audio style transfer [19] matches such statistics too, but optimizes the output signal rather than an input to a fixed generator.\nLayer structure. Self-supervised speech models distribute information unevenly with depth [20]; a layer-wise probe of WavLMLarge puts peak speaker-identification accuracy at layer 4 [21].\n3. METHOD\nThe target deployment pattern is a TTS release that publishes the\nsynthesis model and a set of preset style vectors while keeping the\nreference encoder private. Few public systems fit it: most either\nship the speaker encoder alongside the weights (CosyVoice [22]),\nprompt directly on reference audio with no explicit style vector (F5TTS [23]), or condition on discrete codec tokens (VALL-E [9]). We\nstudy SupertonicTTS, which fits the pattern and is small enough to\ndifferentiate through end to end. Fig. 1 shows the setup.\n3.1. Problem formulation\nLet G be a frozen TTS pipeline mapping text t and a style vector s to\na waveform. For a target recording y∗ we solve\n\u0001\ns∗ = arg min L G(t, s), y∗ ,\n(1)\ns\n\nwhere t is text of our choosing, unrelated to what y∗ says. SupertonicTTS exposes sttl ∈ R50×256 , which enters the acoustic modules as cross-attention keys and values, and a separate duration style\nsdp ∈ R8×16 . We optimize sttl , 12,800 free parameters, and keep sdp\nfixed.\n\n3.3. A content-independent objective\nThe generated and target utterances contain different words, so the\nobjective must ignore content. Let H = [h1 , . . . , hT ] ∈ RT ×D denote\nWavLM-Large layer-4 hidden states, D=1024. We collapse the time\naxis into per-dimension statistics,\nµ = T1 ∑τ hτ ,\n\nσ = std(H),\n\n(2)\n\nand compare them by mean squared error,\n2\n\n2\n\nL = D1 µ ŷ − µ y∗ 2 + D1 σ ŷ − σ y∗ 2 .\n\n(3)\n\nMean-and-standard-deviation pooling over time is the statistics pooling used by x-vector speaker embeddings [24]. More directly, this\nexact descriptor (time-pooled mean and standard deviation of frozen\nSSL hidden states, no trained head) is itself a competent speaker\nrepresentation [16], which is why the objective transfers to speaker\nverification metrics it never saw. Pooling over time also removes\nany dependence on utterance length. We use layer 4, where Chiu et\nal. [21] report peak speaker-identification accuracy for this model;\ntheir layer indexing counts the CNN encoder output as layer 0 and\nso matches ours.\n3.4. Initialization and stopping\nOptimization starts from the preset whose output on the first prompt\nis closest to the target under Eq. 3, over the ten released presets.\nBecause the encoder is unavailable, the extent of the valid style\nregion is unknown, and driving the loss arbitrarily low pushes the\nvector into territory the model was never trained on. We therefore\ncalibrate a stopping point from public material only. Synthesizing\ntwo different sentences from the same preset leaves a residual loss\nattributable to content rather than to speaker mismatch. Across the\nten presets and four prompts, these 60 same-preset pairs span 0.15 to\n0.41 with a mean of 0.27. We stop at 0.30; 37 of the 60 pairs fall below that value, so the stopping point sits near the 62nd percentile of\nthe content-only floor. This floor is measured between two synthesized clips, while optimization compares a synthesized clip to a real\none, and the two are not on the same scale: 0.30 is a calibrated operating point, not a bound on where content ceases to be the residual.\nSection 4.3 shows what happens on either side of it.\n\n3.2. Gradient flow through a deployed graph\nThe released pipeline is four ONNX modules built for inference. We\nsimplify each graph with onnxslim, downgrade the opset from 19\nto 17, and strip trailing empty inputs from Clip operators, after which\nonnx2torch produces a differentiable PyTorch module. Every converted parameter and every WavLM parameter is frozen; the style\nvector is the only tensor in the optimizer.\n\n3.5. Optimization details\nFive English prompts chosen for phoneme coverage rotate across\nsteps so the style vector cannot overfit one phoneme set. We use\nAdam at learning rate 2×10−4 , gradient clipping at max-norm 1.0,\nand ReduceLROnPlateau with patience 200 and factor 0.5. Each\nprompt has its own noise latent, drawn once from a fixed seed and\n\n\fTable 1. 154 speakers, five utterances each. The baseline is the\npreset each speaker was initialized from, synthesized on the same\nprompts.\nSIME ↑ SIMR ↑ SIMW ↑ WER↓\nAll (154)\n\nPreset\nProposed\n\n0.132\n0.413\n\n0.099\n0.401\n\n0.713\n0.836\n\n1.84%\n3.19%\n\nVCTK (110)\n\nPreset\nProposed\n\n0.132\n0.393\n\n0.099\n0.384\n\n0.699\n0.824\n\n1.67%\n3.99%\n\nSeed-TTS (44)\n\nPreset\nProposed\n\n0.132\n0.463\n\n0.099\n0.444\n\n0.747\n0.865\n\n2.28%\n1.19%\n\nreused at every step. That latent is sized once, from a single preset’s predicted duration, and reused for every speaker, so the duration predictor’s output never enters the waveform path and sdp has\nno gradient path to the loss: utterance timing is not recovered here.\nBecause each style vector reaches only its own output, several\nspeakers can be optimized in one forward pass by summing their\nper-speaker losses; a speaker that reaches the threshold is removed\nfrom the batch at once, so it cannot drift past the stopping point.\nAt batch 16 on one RTX 3090 this costs 0.49 minutes per speaker,\nagainst 5 to 6 minutes optimized singly.\n4. EXPERIMENTS\n4.1. Setup\nWe evaluate the public SupertonicTTS 2 release [25] (internal version v1.6.0), whose architecture is described in [1]. It ships 10\npreset styles and no reference encoder.\nTargets are 154 speakers from two corpora with different recording conditions: 110 from VCTK [26] (studio, 8 to 12 s of reference\naudio, real gender and accent metadata) and 44 from the Common\nVoice subset of Seed-TTS Eval [27] (crowdsourced, 3 to 16 s). For\nevery speaker we synthesize the same five prompts used during optimization, from the extracted style and from the preset it started\nfrom, so the baseline is matched. The target recording’s own words\nare never used at any stage.\nSpeaker similarity is cosine similarity between reference and\ngenerated embeddings under ECAPA-TDNN [28] (SIME ) and\nResNet (SIMR ), both from SpeechBrain VoxCeleb checkpoints,\nand under WavLM-base-plus-sv (SIMW ), the metric most TTS work\nreports. SIME and SIMR are trained independently of anything we\noptimize; SIMW shares the WavLM family with our objective, so\nwe report it for comparability rather than as an independent check.\nIntelligibility is word error rate under Whisper-large-v3.\nFor scale, we measured both ends of the ECAPA range on these\nsame references. Different speakers score 0.118 on average (95th\npercentile 0.321, maximum 0.629); two recordings of one VCTK\nspeaker score a median of 0.682; two halves of a single recording,\nwhich also fixes the channel, score 0.804 over the 121 references\nlong enough to split. The nearest preset therefore starts almost at the\nimpostor floor. The impostor tail is long, so a single score near 0.4\nis not by itself proof of identity; the comparison that matters is the\npaired change per speaker.\n4.2. Main results\nTable 1 reports the result against the nearest-preset baseline, the\nstrongest option available to a user restricted to the public release.\n\nSpeaker similarity rises from the impostor floor to about 60% of\nthe 0.682 that two real recordings of one speaker score: SIME from\n0.132 to 0.413 and SIMR from 0.099 to 0.401. Bootstrap 95% confidence intervals over speakers are [0.395, 0.430] and [0.385, 0.418].\nThe gain is universal, not an average over successes and failures.\nEvery one of the 154 speakers ends closer to its target than its own\npreset does, under SIME and SIMR , and 145 of 154 under SIMW .\nThe objective sees none of these three, so the gain is transfer, not the\noptimizer scoring its own work. A Wilcoxon signed-rank test returns\np < 10−26 for both independent metrics, with paired effect sizes dz\nof 2.70 and 2.82. The two verification models also rank speakers\nalmost identically (Spearman ρ = 0.881), so the improvement is not\nan artifact of one embedding space.\nExtraction costs intelligibility but does not destroy it: mean\nWER rises from 1.84% to 3.19%. The mean is misleading here.\nThe median is 0.00% and 107 of the 154 speakers transcribe with no\nerror at all, so the increase is carried by a minority of failures rather\nthan by uniform degradation. Section 4.3 attributes part of that tail\nto the synthesizer itself.\nThe recovered vectors also behave like native ones. Predicted\nnaturalness (UTMOS [29]) reaches 4.23 against 4.47 for the model’s\nown presets, 95% of what the synthesizer attains from a style vector\nit shipped; the median speaker gives up 0.10, at dz = 0.66 against\n2.70 for the similarity gain on the same clips. Staying near the preset\nlevel is evidence that the vector lands inside the region the model\nwas trained on, which is what the stopping rule buys (Section 4.3).\nUTMOS is trained on systems from earlier challenges, so it bounds\nthis rather than measuring naturalness.\nThe two corpora differ: Seed-TTS speakers reach higher similarity (0.463 against 0.393) and their WER falls rather than rises,\nwhile VCTK speakers gain less despite converging faster (297 steps\nagainst 568). They differ in recording condition, duration, and accent coverage at once, so we offer no controlled explanation.\n4.3. Where to stop\nThe stopping threshold is the one hyperparameter with no principled\nsetting available from the release alone, so we characterize what\nit buys. Continuing six speakers from 0.30 down to 0.24, below\nthe calibrated stopping point, raises SIME from 0.498 to 0.534 but\nlifts word error rate from 1.07% to 5.22% and leaves SIMW slightly\nlower, 0.910 against 0.906. Past that point the optimizer is buying\nspeaker similarity with intelligibility. Two of the six speakers account for nearly all the damage, one going from 0.00% to 18.85%\nWER.\nSome of that fragility is the synthesizer’s, not ours. Synthesizing\nthe ten stock presets with no optimization at all, over 250 clips, gives\na mean WER of 0.38%, but two clips exceed 10% and one presetsentence pair swings 15 points across noise seeds. Any single-clip\nWER from this model, including ours, carries that variance.\n4.4. Would a verifier accept these voices?\nA cosine similarity is hard to read as a risk. Table 2 restates the result\nas a verification decision, in the form a spoofing evaluation would\nuse: with the verifier tuned to a given false-accept rate on genuine\nand impostor trials, how often does it accept an extracted voice as\nthe target speaker? On this data the verifier separates real speakers\ncleanly, with an equal-error rate of 0.9% at a threshold of 0.406.\nAt that operating point it accepts 52.6% of the extracted voices and\n1.3% of the presets they were built from. Loosening to a 5% falseaccept rate raises acceptance to 85.7%, against 9.1% for the presets.\n\n\f(a) convergence, 154 speakers\n\n0.8\n\nspeakers\n\nWavLM layer-4 loss\n\n1.0\n\n0.6\nmean\n\n0.4\n0.2\n\n0\n\n200\n\n400\n\nstopping threshold 0.3\n\n600\n800\noptimization step\n\n1000\n\n(b) speaker similarity, 154 speakers\n\n30\n\n1200\n\nnearest preset\nproposed\n\n20\n10\n0\n\n0.0\n\n0.2\n0.4\nSIME (ECAPA-TDNN)\n\n0.6\n\nFig. 2. (a) Best-so-far loss for all 154 speakers; light curves are individual speakers, the dark curve their mean. Every speaker reaches the\n0.30 threshold, on average after 375 steps and between 80 and 1275. (b) ECAPA similarity before and after extraction; the two distributions\nbarely overlap.\n\nTable 2. Acceptance by an ECAPA verifier tuned to a given falseaccept rate. “Genuine” is a second real recording of the same\nspeaker, which is what a deployed verifier actually sees; those trials\ncover the 110 VCTK speakers, the only ones with a second recording, against 6000 impostor pairs over all 154 references.\nOperating point\n\nThresh.\n\nGenuine\n\nExtracted\n\nPreset\n\nEER (0.9%)\nFAR = 5%\nFAR = 1%\nFAR = 0.1%\n\n0.406\n0.290\n0.399\n0.519\n\n99.1%\n100.0%\n100.0%\n92.7%\n\n52.6%\n85.7%\n53.9%\n18.8%\n\n1.3%\n9.1%\n1.9%\n0.0%\n\n4.5. How accurate is preset selection?\nVCTK ships per-speaker gender labels, which lets us score the initialization against ground truth rather than against itself. The nearest\npreset matches the labeled gender for 105 of 110 speakers (95.5%).\nAll five errors share one direction: a male preset (M1) chosen for\na female speaker. Initialization is therefore good but not reliable,\nwhich matters because the starting point is the one part of the procedure with no feedback signal.\n5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\nWhat initialization does. Preset initialization is a convenience, not\na requirement. In an early three-speaker pilot, run at layer 3 with\nthree prompts and a 0.24 stopping loss, a zero vector reaches SIME\n0.496 against 0.594 for the standard start; on the two speakers also\nrun from an opposite-category preset, the mean falls from 0.593 to\n0.460. All are far above the 0.13 baseline, so optimization recovers\nfrom a poor start rather than failing. That pilot predates the settings\nabove and scores above the 154-speaker mean, so only its internal\nordering is meaningful.\nLayer choice. We adopt the layer that [21] reports as best for\nspeaker identity rather than sweeping layers ourselves. Neighboring\nearly layers plausibly work as well; the loss scale differs between\nthem, so each would need its own stopping threshold.\nDuration style. The latent length is fixed in our graph, so sdp\nhas no gradient path to the loss and speaking rate is inherited from\nthe initializing preset. Recovering it needs a latent whose length\nfollows the duration predictor, which we leave open.\n\nNo countermeasure evaluation. We measure acceptance by a\nspeaker verifier, not by a spoofing countermeasure. Synthetic speech\nfrom this model may well be detectable even when the verifier accepts it: our result bounds what identity information the conditioning space carries, not whether the audio survives a detector. Pairing\nextraction with a countermeasure benchmark is the natural next experiment.\nScope. Results come from one TTS model. The method assumes only a differentiable pipeline and a continuous style input, so\nit should transfer, but we have not shown that. The five evaluation\nprompts are the ones optimized on, so generalization to unseen text\nis untested. Naturalness is only a predicted score; a listening test remains necessary. Accent coverage is uneven and the weakest results\nfall on the least represented accents.\n6. CONCLUSION\nWe recover a withheld conditioning input from a released synthesizer by optimization alone. Without an encoder there is no supervision signal for the fit; we substitute a time-pooled early-layer\nWavLM statistic, which makes the comparison content-independent,\nso one untranscribed recording suffices and it need not say anything in particular. A stopping point calibrated from the released\npresets keeps the result inside the region the model was trained on.\nAcross 154 speakers the method raises ECAPA speaker similarity\nfrom 0.132 to 0.413, improves every speaker, and costs half a minute\nof GPU time each; a verifier at its equal-error point accepts 53% of\nthe results as the target, against 1% for the presets they were derived\nfrom.\nOne implication is that withholding the encoder removes a convenience rather than a capability. Whatever a partial release is meant\nto achieve, it should be assessed against what the released components already determine, not against the intended interface alone.\n7. COMPLIANCE WITH ETHICAL STANDARDS\nUse of a released model outside its intended interface. This work\noperates a publicly released model in a way its authors did not design for: SupertonicTTS ships synthesis weights and preset voices,\nthe reference encoder is withheld, and we reach the conditioning\nspace without it. We used only public artifacts, breached no access\n\n\fcontrol, and modified no released file, but operating a release outside its intended interface carries obligations beyond its license, so\nwe state plainly what we did.\nDual use. The measurement is directly usable to imitate a real\nperson. That is not a side effect: the quantity we report is how convincingly a recovered vector reproduces a specific voice, so a high\nnumber is what makes misuse feasible. We report it because that\nsame number tells a provider, or a user relying on one, how much\nof a voice a partial release already determines. A result of this kind\ncannot be reported in a form that informs the defense but not the\noffense.\nData, approval and funding. All evaluation uses consented,\nopenly licensed corpora: VCTK (CC BY 4.0) and the Common\nVoice subset of Seed-TTS Eval (CC0). The study was conducted\nretrospectively on human-subject speech data already available in\nopen access; no new recordings were collected, and ethical approval\nwas not required, as confirmed by the licenses attached to that data.\nNo identifiable individual is targeted, no public figure appears, and\nwe release no extracted style vector for any real person. The released\ncode documents that cloning a voice without the speaker’s consent\nis out of scope for it. This work received no external funding, and\nthe author declares no conflict of interest.\nWhat we release. We publish the procedure in enough detail to\nreproduce the measurement, with the code that produced the numbers. We do not package it as a turnkey cloning tool, and the technique it builds on has been public since 2018 [3–5], so the marginal\ncapability this adds is small relative to the transparency it buys.\nMitigations available to providers. The conditioning space is\nreachable because it is continuous, differentiable, and shipped alongside working examples. Perturbing or quantizing the released presets, watermarking synthesizer output so generated speech remains\nattributable, or serving the acoustic modules behind an interface that\ndoes not expose gradients would each raise the cost. We measured\nnone of these and offer them as directions, not results.\n8. REFERENCES\n[1] H. Kim, J. Yang, Y. Yu, S. Ji, J. Morton, F. Bous, J. Byun, and\nJ. Lee, “SupertonicTTS: Towards highly efficient and streamlined text-to-speech system,” arXiv:2503.23108, 2025.\n[2] M. Fredrikson, S. Jha, and T. Ristenpart, “Model inversion\nattacks that exploit confidence information and basic countermeasures,” in Proc. ACM CCS, 2015, pp. 1322–1333.\n[3] Y. Taigman et al., “VoiceLoop: Voice fitting and synthesis via\na phonological loop,” in Proc. ICLR, 2018.\n[4] S. Ö. Arik et al., “Neural voice cloning with a few samples,”\nin Proc. NeurIPS, 2018, pp. 10040–10050.\n[5] Y. Chen et al., “Sample efficient adaptive text-to-speech,” in\nProc. ICLR, 2019.\n[6] R. Abdal, Y. Qin, and P. Wonka, “Image2StyleGAN++: How\nto edit the embedded images?,” in Proc. CVPR, 2020, pp.\n8296–8305.\n[7] J. Lin, G. Sharma, and T. N. Pappas, “Texture representation via analysis and synthesis with generative adversarial networks,” arXiv:2212.09983, 2022.\n[8] Y. Jia et al., “Transfer learning from speaker verification\nto multispeaker text-to-speech synthesis,” in Proc. NeurIPS,\n2018, pp. 4485–4495.\n[9] C. Wang et al., “Neural codec language models are zero-shot\ntext to speech synthesizers,” arXiv:2301.02111, 2023.\n\n[10] M. Le et al., “Voicebox: Text-guided multilingual universal\nspeech generation at scale,” in Proc. NeurIPS, 2023.\n[11] W. Jin et al., “Towards evaluating the robustness of automatic speech recognition systems via audio style transfer,”\narXiv:2405.09470, 2024.\n[12] X. Gao et al., “TTSlow: Slow down text-to-speech with efficiency robustness evaluations,” arXiv:2407.01927, 2024.\n[13] M. Marras et al., “Dictionary attacks on speaker verification,” IEEE Trans. Inf. Forensics Security, vol. 18, pp. 773–\n788, 2023.\n[14] M. Baas, B. van Niekerk, and H. Kamper, “Voice conversion\nwith just nearest neighbors,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2023, pp.\n2053–2057.\n[15] K. El Hajal et al., “kNN retrieval for simple and effective zeroshot multi-speaker text-to-speech,” in Proc. NAACL, 2025, pp.\n778–786.\n[16] Z. Aldeneh et al., “Can you remove the downstream model for\nspeaker recognition with self-supervised speech features?,” in\nProc. Interspeech, 2024, pp. 4648–4652.\n[17] R. Abdal, Y. Qin, and P. Wonka, “Image2StyleGAN: How\nto embed images into the StyleGAN latent space?,” in Proc.\nICCV, 2019, pp. 4432–4441.\n[18] R. Gal et al., “An image is worth one word: Personalizing textto-image generation using textual inversion,” in Proc. ICLR,\n2023.\n[19] E. Grinstein, N. Q. K. Duong, A. Ozerov, and P. Pérez, “Audio\nstyle transfer,” in Proc. ICASSP, 2018, pp. 586–590.\n[20] A. Pasad, J.-C. Chou, and K. Livescu, “Layer-wise analysis of\na self-supervised speech representation model,” in Proc. IEEE\nASRU, 2021, pp. 914–921.\n[21] A. Y. F. Chiu et al., “A large-scale probing analysis of speakerspecific attributes in self-supervised speech representations,”\narXiv:2501.05310v3, 2026.\n[22] Z. Du et al., “CosyVoice: A scalable multilingual zero-shot\ntext-to-speech synthesizer based on supervised semantic tokens,” arXiv:2407.05407, 2024.\n[23] Y. Chen et al., “F5-TTS: A fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching,” in Proc. ACL, 2025, pp. 6255–\n6271.\n[24] D. Snyder et al., “X-vectors: Robust DNN embeddings for\nspeaker recognition,” in Proc. ICASSP, 2018, pp. 5329–5333.\n[25] Supertone Inc.,\n“Supertonic 2 open-weight release,”\n2026. [Online]. Available: https://huggingface.co/\nSupertone/supertonic-2.\n[26] J. Yamagishi, C. Veaux, and K. MacDonald, “CSTR VCTK\ncorpus: English multi-speaker corpus for CSTR voice cloning\ntoolkit (version 0.92),” Univ. of Edinburgh, 2019.\n[27] P. Anastassiou et al., “Seed-TTS: A family of high-quality\nversatile speech generation models,” arXiv:2406.02430, 2024.\n[28] B. Desplanques, J. Thienpondt, and K. Demuynck, “ECAPATDNN: Emphasized channel attention, propagation and aggregation in TDNN based speaker verification,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2020, pp. 3830–3834.\n[29] T. Saeki et al., “UTMOS: UTokyo-SaruLab system for VoiceMOS Challenge 2022,” in Proc. Interspeech, 2022, pp. 4521–\n4525.\n\n\f", "numLines": 589, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 589}}}
